import json, sys
OBJ = """WeirHook: a Uniswap v4 hook for a NEW WEIR/IMD pool on Robinhood Chain (chain id 4663) that takes 0.5% of every swap, always in IMD, and hands it to the live Weir contract's court fund through Weir.sponsor(). Two contracts, Launcher and WeirHook. Nothing is upgradeable, pausable or ownable; no admin exists anywhere; the hook can never hold IMD after collect() and nobody can withdraw from it. The hook's rate is 0.5%. No backend signer.

ADDRESSES (static constructor words, 4663). WEIR 0x58b82fa656ecf47a0dca5be6bad06b92e22f7dbe (plain ERC-20, 18 decimals, fixed 1,000,000,000 supply). IMD 0x5f7bb59365ce557c26dbcaa4ee9d39a4b95b7127 (ERC-20, 18 decimals, has an owner with a blocklist; treat as untrusted, never assume a bool return). Uniswap v4 PoolManager on Robinhood 0x8366a39CC670B4001A1121B8F6A443A643e40951 (non-canonical deployment, verified live). Weir court 0x320de26e4bc988e0c425531e564ded3d2f730d32 (live; its function sponsor(uint256 imdAmount) pulls IMD with transferFrom(msg.sender) and credits the court fund; it has no other role here). Both tokens are ERC-20: WEIR is currency0 and IMD currency1 (sorted by address). A factory pool WEIR/IMD already exists (fee 12500, tickSpacing 60, hook 0x19bec7c2e1b2aadaf67b259744751a9960d66000); this launch opens a DIFFERENT pool with the WeirHook and must not touch the existing one.

CONSTRUCTORS take static words only (address, uint, bool, bytes32: the deployment manifest supports nothing else, no arrays, no int24), make no external calls and read nothing on-chain (the verifier deploys in an empty EVM). Launcher constructor args: weir, imd, poolManager, court (four addresses, nothing else). EVERY OTHER NUMBER IS A CODE CONSTANT: tickSpacing 60 (int24 constant), lpFee 3000 (0.30%, static pool fee), hookCut 5000 in hundredths of a bip (0.50%), IMD decimals 18. The WeirHook is created by the Launcher with CREATE2 and takes (weir, imd, poolManager, court) too; it must NOT validate its own address bits in its constructor; the Launcher checks them after CREATE2. The Launcher exposes initCodeHash() (view) and hookAddress(bytes32 salt) (view) so the salt can be mined off-chain.

LAUNCHER. One permissionless function open(bytes32 salt, uint160 sqrtPriceX96) that succeeds once: (1) deploys the WeirHook with CREATE2 and reverts unless its address carries exactly the permission bits for beforeSwap, afterSwap, beforeSwapReturnDelta and afterSwapReturnDelta and no others (low 14 bits = 0x00CC); (2) initializes the WEIR/IMD pool on the PoolManager with lpFee, tickSpacing and the WeirHook as hook at sqrtPriceX96; emits Opened(hook, poolId). If somebody already initialized that exact pool key before open() ran, open() adopts the pool at its live price instead of reverting and emits Preinitialized(poolId, livePrice, requestedPrice). No liquidity is added by the Launcher: the deployer adds a WEIR-only range position later through the normal v4 PositionManager, so the hook must not restrict liquidity in any way (no liquidity callbacks, no beforeInitialize binding beyond the pool key check in the swap callbacks).

WEIRHOOK, CUT. On every swap in its pool the hook takes hookCut of the swap's IMD side as its cut, ALWAYS IN IMD, on top of the pool's static lpFee: when IMD is the input, from the input (beforeSwap return delta on the specified currency for exact-input, afterSwap on the unspecified for exact-output); when WEIR is the input, from the IMD output (afterSwap return delta for exact-input, beforeSwap for exact-output). Work out each of the four cases so the trader is charged hookCut of the IMD side and the pool's accounting settles. The cut is NOT taken as real tokens inside the swap (the trader's input is settled by the router only after the swap, so poolManager.take would revert on an empty manager): the hook settles its return delta by MINTING ERC-6909 claim tokens for IMD to itself (poolManager.mint(address(this), imd, amount)) and adds the amount to `claims`. The hook must accept swaps and quotes from ANY router: no router allow-list, no OnlySwapRouter check, no msg.sender check beyond the PoolManager, so the Universal Router, the Quoter and the StateView all work. collect(): permissionless; burns the hook's whole IMD claim balance and takes real IMD out of the PoolManager (poolManager.unlock -> burn + take, or the equivalent), approves the court for exactly that amount and calls court.sponsor(amount) in the same call, so the hook's IMD balance is 0 afterwards and `collected` grows by the amount; a collect() with nothing to collect is a harmless no-op. If court.sponsor reverts (IMD blocklist), collect() reverts and the claims stay in the hook; nothing else can move them. Views: claims(), collected(), poolKey(), poolId(), hookCut(). Events Cut(trader, imdTaken) per swap (trader = tx.origin is NOT needed; use the swap's sender argument) and Collected(amount) per collect(). No fee tiers, no pass, no owner, no withdraw.

TESTS against the real v4 PoolManager (vendor v4-core and v4-periphery test routers) with mock WEIR and IMD (18 decimals) and a MockCourt whose sponsor(amount) pulls with transferFrom and records the total: open() once and only at an address with the right bits; a salt whose address lacks the bits reverts; a second open() reverts; a WEIR-only range position above the opening price added through the test liquidity router, and a two-sided position too; swaps in all four cases (WEIR in / IMD in, exact in / exact out) through the test swap router, checking the IMD cut equals hookCut of the IMD side within rounding, that the cut shows in claims() and after collect() in the MockCourt's total and that the hook's IMD balance and claim balance are 0 after collect(), that collect() with nothing to collect is a no-op, and that the trader also paid lpFee; the pre-initialized adoption path; a reverting court leaves the claims in place; nobody can withdraw; fuzz the swap amounts. README with the rules, the exact address-mining recipe (read launcher.initCodeHash(), mine a salt so the CREATE2 address keccak256(0xff ++ launcher ++ salt ++ initCodeHash)[12:] ends in hook bits 0x00CC, check it with launcher.hookAddress(salt), then call launcher.open(salt, sqrtPriceX96) from any account, with sqrtPriceX96 read from the existing factory pool's slot0), the liquidity step, and the caveat that the court fund it feeds can be spent by any Weir dispute. BUILD: solidity 0.8.26, optimizer true, optimizer_runs 200, via_ir = true, evm_version cancun, bytecode_hash = "none" in [profile.default] (README quotes foundry.toml verbatim), custom errors only, no ReentrancyGuard (external token calls last), WeirHook deployed code under 12,000 bytes. Slither: multiply before dividing; string.concat not encodePacked. SPDX MIT. README says the project is not audited beyond the swarm's panel and uses no yield, return or investment language."""

req = {
  "action": "launch.open",
  "input": {
    "objective": OBJ,
    "shape": "chain",
    "references": ["uniswap-v4-hooks", "uniswap-v4-security", "eth-security", "eth-testing", "pashov-skill"],
    "steps": [
      {"skill": "build-contract-project",
       "acceptanceCriteria": [
         "Only the Launcher is in launch.json, with four static address constructor args (weir, imd, poolManager, court) and no $owner; the WeirHook is created by open() with CREATE2 and must carry exactly the 0x00CC permission bits",
         "open() succeeds once, initializes a WEIR/IMD pool with lpFee 3000 and tickSpacing 60 (constants) and adopts a pre-initialized pool instead of reverting",
         "In all four swap cases the hook takes 0.5% (hookCut 5000 hundredths of a bip) of the IMD side, in IMD, by minting ERC-6909 claims to itself; no router restriction of any kind",
         "collect() is permissionless, burns and takes the whole claim balance and hands it to court.sponsor() in the same call; the hook holds no IMD afterwards and has no other way to move value",
         "No owner, admin, pause, upgrade, liquidity callback or withdraw path; constructors read nothing and call nothing",
         "foundry.toml sets bytecode_hash = \"none\" with the pinned solc, optimizer, evm_version and via_ir; README quotes it, documents the salt-mining and liquidity steps, says not audited, no yield or return language"
       ]},
      {"skill": "write-foundry-tests", "paths": ["test/**"]},
      {"skill": "adversarial-review"}
    ],
    "onchain": "evm_contracts",
    "chainId": 4663,
    "github": True
  }
}
s = json.dumps(req, separators=(",", ":"))
crit = sum(len(c) for c in req["input"]["steps"][0]["acceptanceCriteria"])
print("objective", len(OBJ), "criteria", crit, "rendered step1 ~", len(OBJ)+crit, "body bytes", len(s.encode()))
json.dump(req, open(sys.argv[1], "w"), indent=2); print("wrote", sys.argv[1])
